  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 13,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.449,
            0.97,
            0.55,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.741,
            0.047,
            0.862,
            0.063
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Quadrilaterals"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.088,
            0.461,
            0.106
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Consider a quadrilateral SOME."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.107,
            0.554,
            0.143
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Draw a diagonal SM. We get two triangles ∆SEM and ∆SOM."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.143,
            0.526,
            0.161
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In ∆SEM, we have "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle 1 + \\angle 2 + \\angle 3 = 180^{\\circ}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.162,
            0.486,
            0.179
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "And in ∆SOM, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle 4 + \\angle 5 + \\angle 6 = 180^{\\circ}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.18,
            0.556,
            0.217
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What do we get when we add all six angles?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.217,
            0.284,
            0.234
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We will have"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.234,
            0.553,
            0.27
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_13_equation_8.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}\\angle 1 + \\angle 2 + \\angle 3 + \\angle 4 + \\angle 5 + \\angle 6 &= 180^{\\circ} + 180^{\\circ} \\\\&= 360^{\\circ}.\\end{aligned}"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.56,
            0.078,
            0.848,
            0.276
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 9,
              "bbox": [
                0.56,
                0.078,
                0.848,
                0.276
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_13_image_9.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.271,
            0.546,
            0.289
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Or, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\left( \\angle 1 + \\angle 4 \\right) + \\left( \\angle 3 + \\angle 6 \\right) + \\angle 2 + \\angle 5 = 360^{\\circ}."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.29,
            0.846,
            0.328
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Since "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( \\angle 1 + \\angle 4 ) , ( \\angle 3 + \\angle 6 ) , \\angle 2   and   \\angle 5"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " are the angles of this quadrilateral, we have the following result—"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.331,
            0.659,
            0.351
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The sum of all angles in any quadrilateral is 360°.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.355,
            0.847,
            0.395
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This explains why it is impossible for a quadrilateral to have three right angles, with the fourth angle not right angle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.409,
            0.794,
            0.457
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4.3 More Quadrilaterals with Parallel Opposite Sides"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.467,
            0.77,
            0.488
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Rectangles (and therefore squares) have parallel opposite sides."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.497,
            0.846,
            0.537
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Are there quadrilaterals that have parallel opposite sides that are not rectangles?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.542,
            0.421,
            0.56
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us try constructing one."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.559,
            0.848,
            0.599
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This can be easily done by drawing two pairs of parallel lines, ensuring that they do not meet at right angles."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.607,
            0.847,
            0.629
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Construct such a figure by recalling how parallel lines can be"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.628,
            0.59,
            0.648
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "constructed using a ruler and a set-square,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.647,
            0.41,
            0.665
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "or a compass and a ruler."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.702,
            0.59,
            0.741
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Observe the quadrilateral ABCD. It has parallel opposite sides but is not a rectangle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 23,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.777,
            0.591,
            0.835
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Thus, a larger set of quadrilaterals exists in which the opposite sides are parallel. Such quadrilaterals are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "parallelograms.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 24,
          "bbox": [
            0.597,
            0.641,
            0.863,
            0.797
          ],
          "content": [
